\section{Materials and methods}\label{sec:methods}

\subsection{Study design and estimands}\label{sec:design}

To compare arrays without committing to a particular detector, we used the known-topography detectability of each source. The known-topography detectability is the output SNR of the matched filter that knows both the field pattern of the source and the covariance of the noise,
\begin{equation}
d = \sqrt{\symbf{s}^{\top}\symbf{C}^{-1}\symbf{s}}.
\label{eq:d}
\end{equation}
Here, $\symbf{s}$ is the noise-free field of a << F.g2_focal_nam >>-nAm cortical dipole at the channels of the array. The matrix $\symbf{C}$ is the noise covariance of the array (Section~\ref{sec:noise}), taken as known, i.e., the true covariance of the modeled noise (the oracle covariance). Two arrays were then compared target by target, by the ratio $d_{\mathrm{OPM}}/d_{\mathrm{Neuromag}}$ or, in decibels, by
\begin{equation}
D = 20\log_{10}\frac{d_{\mathrm{OPM}}}{d_{\mathrm{Neuromag}}},
\label{eq:D}
\end{equation}
which is positive when the OPM array has the higher detectability.

Each comparison was then summarized over the cortex. For the adult, we took the unweighted median of the target-wise ratios over all << F.g2_n_targets >> targets, including the << F.g2_medialwall_n >> vertices on the medial wall. We set this convention for the adult before the smaller heads were added, and we retained it rather than change it after the results were known. For the dense array, excluding these vertices would change the median ratio to << F.dp("wr_g2_dense_ib_ratio_no_medial_wall", 2) >>, and the area-weighted convention of the smaller heads would give << F.dp("wr_adult_dense_ib_ratio_area_weighted_cortical_3dp", 2) >> (<< F.g3b_link_g2_db >>~dB). Because the smaller heads differ in cortical area, we summarized each of them by the area-weighted median of $D$ over its cortical targets, with the medial wall excluded.

To compare a smaller head with the adult, we placed both in the same helmet and pose (Section~\ref{sec:arrays}) and computed the change from the adult, $\Delta$, from paired differences rather than as the difference of their medians. How the differences were paired depended on the anatomy. For the scaled adults, which are downscaled copies of the adult and share its vertices, $\Delta$ was computed as the median of $D_{\mathrm{head}}(v) - D_{\mathrm{adult}}(v)$ over common vertices, weighted by the target areas of the smaller head. For the other heads, which have their own cortical surfaces and therefore do not share the adult's vertices, the area-weighted median of $D$ was first taken within every parcel of the Desikan--Killiany atlas that contains at least << F.g3b_cfg_min_n >> targets in both heads. In the << F.g3b_infant2yr_age_months >>- and << F.g3b_infant12mo_age_months >>-month templates, << F.g3b_infant2yr_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_n >> and << F.g3b_infant12mo_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_n >> parcels, respectively, met this criterion. The change from the adult was then computed as the median of the parcel differences, weighted by the adult's parcel areas.

\subsection{Head models}\label{sec:heads}

We used the MNE sample subject as the adult head \citep{gramfort2013}, with its FreeSurfer surfaces and atlas labels \citep{desikan2006,fischl2012}. The targets were the vertices of an MNE-Python oct-6 source space on the white-matter surface, each modeled as a << F.g2_focal_nam >>-nAm dipole normal to the cortex. Only vertices at least << F.g2_source_skull_clearance_mm >>~mm inside the inner skull were retained. The depth of a target was defined as its distance to the nearest point of the MRI scalp. Forward fields were computed with a three-layer boundary-element model (BEM; \citealp{hamalainen1989}) with conductivities of << F.g2_conductivity >>~S/m, whose outer surface was the MRI scalp. The BEM was solved by linear collocation, with << F.g2_head_triangles_coarse >> triangles per skull surface and the scalp refined to << F.g2_head_triangles >> triangles. We checked this BEM in two ways. First, compared with the field of an exact three-shell sphere, the field of this BEM had a 95th-percentile error of << F.g2_bemsphere_20480_h2mm_p95_pct >> at 2~mm and << F.g2_bemsphere_20480_h15mm_p95_pct >> at 15~mm outside the outer surface. Second, on a subset of targets, we computed the ratio of the dense array with a scalp of << F.g2_head_triangles_coarse >> triangles and with the refined scalp of << F.g2_head_triangles >> triangles. The refinement changed this ratio from << F.g2_skin_bem3_5120_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio_3dp >> to << F.g2_skin_bem3_skin20480_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio_3dp >>.

Of the << F.wr_n_smaller_heads_words >> smaller heads (Table~\ref{tab:anatomies}), four form the basis of the main analysis, and we refer to them as the principal smaller heads. << F.wr_n_scaled_words|capitalize >> of them were made by scaling the adult, one to school-age size and the other to the head circumference of the << F.g3b_infant2yr_age_months >>-month template. Because these scaled adults preserve the adult anatomy exactly, they are the main test of head size and helmet fit. The other << F.wr_n_principal_templates_words >> are the << F.g3b_infant2yr_age_months >>- and << F.g3b_infant12mo_age_months >>-month infant templates, i.e., averages of many MRIs \citep{oreilly2021,richards2016}, which add infant head shapes to the analysis, subject to the caveat about their scalp convention given below. None of the anatomies, however, is an independent replication, because all of them share the noise calibration, the adult's conductivities and the processing.

The remaining << F.wr_n_provisional_words >> heads are reported separately in Section~S10. The << F.g3b_infant18mo_age_months >>-month template was classed as << F.wr_qc_infant18mo_verdict >> by an MRI check, because its white surface fitted its T1-weighted image best after a << F.wr_qc_infant18mo_white_best_shift_mm >>-mm shift. In the other two templates, the corresponding shift was << F.wr_qc_templates_other_white_best_shift_mm >>~mm. The MRIs of << F.wr_n_children_words >> typically developing school-aged children, A, B and C (<< F.g3b_children_age_range >> years), were taken from OpenNeuro dataset ds005234 \citep{fadeev2024,fadeev2025}. Their anatomy was prepared in three further steps (Section~S4). First, because in the dataset the inner skull of these children was a median of << F.g3b_children_watershed_inner_skull_depth_p50_range >>~mm below the scalp over the upper head, we displaced it inward, toward << F.g3b_cfg_child_skull_depth >>~mm below the scalp, without bringing it closer than << F.g3b_cfg_child_skull_min_cortex >>~mm to the white-surface vertices. Second, we transferred their fiducials from the adult by a similarity transform fitted between the white surfaces. Third, we checked their surfaces against their T1-weighted MRIs (Fig.~S6). The surfaces were found to be registered to the images, but the check cannot verify the cortex within a few millimeters of the scalp. Because the << F.g2_source_skull_clearance_mm >>-mm limit on targets also left out << F.wr_excl_children_lt8mm_pct_range >> of the children's cortex within 8~mm of the scalp, their results do not describe a near-scalp cortex.

We made two further checks of the principal heads. First, the infant templates follow a different scalp convention from the adult's: their scalps are << F.wr_qc_principal_templates_offset_outside_otsu_mm_range >>~mm outside their own MRI head boundaries, whereas the adult's scalp is << F.wr_qc_adult_offset_inside_otsu_mm >>~mm inside its boundary, a difference of << F.wr_qc_principal_template_adult_convention_mm_range >>~mm between the conventions (Section~S4.3). Second, replacing the three-layer BEM with a single-compartment BEM, which has no skull or scalp conductivity layer, changed the difference between the median $D$ of each principal head and that of the adult by << F.meth_principal_bem1_change_db_range >>~dB. This check tests the conductivity layers, not the position of the scalp or the inner skull.

