\subsection{Sensor arrays and helmets}\label{sec:arrays}

We modeled the Neuromag system (<< F.g2_squid_channels >> channels; ``Neuromag'' below) with the sensor geometry of the sample recording and the T3 coil definitions of MNE-Python (types << F.meth_coil_grad_type >> and << F.meth_coil_mag_type >>). Each gradiometer was integrated at << F.meth_coil_grad_points >> points and each magnetometer at << F.meth_coil_mag_points >>. Neuromag's white noise was set to the typical value of the TRIUX datasheet, << F.meth_squid_mag_asd >>~fT/√Hz for the magnetometers and << F.meth_squid_grad_asd >>~fT/(cm~√Hz) for the gradiometers \citep{elekta_triux}. The Neuromag coils were << F.meth_dewar_mm >>~mm outward from the inner surface of the helmet (the Dewar spacing), and no signal-space separation was applied. We compared each OPM array with all << F.g2_squid_channels >> Neuromag channels and, as alternative comparators, with the << F.g2_squid_grad_channels >> gradiometers or the << F.g2_squid_sites >> magnetometers alone. Besides detectability, we computed two sensor-level measures, the peak-channel SNR and the mean-power SNR \citep{goldenholz2009}, which are defined in Table~\ref{tab:estimands}.

Each OPM was modeled as a single-axis sensor whose sensing center was nominally << F.g2_dense_standoff_mm >>~mm above the MRI scalp \citep{alem2023,osborne2018}, and sites whose sensor cell would otherwise have come within << F.g2_cell_clearance_rule_mm >>~mm of the head surface were displaced outward. In the adult, << F.g2_dense_moved_out >> of the << F.g2_dense_sites >> dense sites and << F.g2_matched_moved_out >> of the << F.g2_matched_sites >> site-matched sites were displaced in this way. The field of each OPM was averaged over a << F.g2_opm_cell_mm >>-mm cube with a << F.meth_opm_cell_points >>-point Gauss--Legendre rule, a modeling choice that changes the peak field by at most << F.wr_opm_cell_peak_effect_pct >>. The sensitive axis was taken as the head-surface normal, averaged within << F.meth_opm_axis_radius_mm >>~mm of the site. Had nearly radial axes been used instead, the ratio of the dense array with the room field projected out would have been << F.dp("meth_hse_radial_dense_proj_ratio_3dp", 2) >> rather than << F.dp("meth_hse_final_dense_proj_ratio_3dp", 2) >> (Sections~\ref{sec:noise} and S3). We set the OPM white noise to << F.g2_opm_asd_primary >>~fT/√Hz in the primary analysis, flat over << F.g2_band_range_hz >>~Hz, and varied it over << F.wr_opm_asd_sweep_list >>~fT/√Hz.

Both OPM arrays were restricted to the same region of the scalp: the part above the plane through the preauricular points and a point << F.wr_cover_brow_offset_mm >>~mm above the nasion, with no OPM within << F.g2_preauricular_exclusion_mm >>~mm of either preauricular point. The dense array was packed into this region at a minimum spacing of << F.g2_dense_packing_mm >>~mm, which gave << F.g2_dense_sites >> sites on the adult, more than any clinical OPM array in the studies of Table~S19. For the site-matched array, one OPM was placed at each Neuromag site whose projection onto the scalp fell inside the region, << F.g2_matched_sites >> of the << F.g2_squid_sites >> sites (Fig.~\ref{fig:arrays}). Clinical systems, in contrast, reach about 100--128 channels with dual- or triaxial sensors at fewer sites \citep{feys2025,shen2026}. We did not model such arrays. Neither single-axis array represents one, because both differ from clinical arrays in site count and sensing axes. To test the effect of extra axes, however, we added a triaxial array at the site-matched sites (<< F.g2_triax_channels >> channels).

In the fixed adult helmet, the adult's sensors and noise were used unchanged for every head (Fig.~\ref{fig:helmets}). For the primary placement in this helmet, each head was first posed with its head coordinate frame where the adult's had been recorded. It was then raised until the nearest magnetometer coil was << F.g3b_cfg_clearance >>~mm from the scalp, a best-fit assumption that favors Neuromag \citep{gaetz2008,tesan2010}. We call this placement top contact. To describe the effect of placement, we also used a family of << F.g3b_n_family_placements >> source-blind placements, i.e., placements chosen from the geometry alone (contacts, shifts and rotations; Section~S5). The OPM arrays, in contrast, were rebuilt on every smaller head: the dense array by the same coverage and spacing rules, and the site-matched array from the Neuromag sites of that head at top contact (Table~\ref{tab:anatomies}).

To separate head size from helmet fit, we also placed each head in a helmet fitted at the adult's gap. The helmet was fitted in two steps. First, each head was translated laterally from the recorded pose onto the midline of the helmet, without being raised to contact (the laterally centered pose). Second, the helmet was scaled about the head origin until the median magnetometer-to-scalp gap equaled that of the adult in the same pose, << F.rev_cgap_adult_gap_mm >>~mm. For comparison, the adult's gap is << F.g2_squid_scalp_dist_median_mm >>~mm at its measured head position and << F.rev_cgap_adult_fixed_gap_mm >>~mm at top contact. No coil was allowed within the << F.g3b_cfg_dewar_spacing >>-mm Dewar spacing of the scalp, a limit that was not reached in any principal head. The fitted helmet retains the adult's sensors and therefore does not model a pediatric SQUID system.

Our primary measure of helmet fit is the paired change, from the adult to a smaller head, in how much detectability Neuromag loses in the fixed helmet relative to the helmet fitted at the adult's gap. We call this measure the interaction. For each target, the contrast between the two helmets within one head is
\begin{equation}
F(v) = D_{\mathrm{fixed}}(v) - D_{\mathrm{fitted}}(v) = 20\log_{10}\frac{d_{\mathrm{Neuromag,\,fitted}}(v)}{d_{\mathrm{Neuromag,\,fixed}}(v)},
\label{eq:F}
\end{equation}
in which the detectability of the OPM cancels. Because the fitted helmet is built about the laterally centered pose rather than at contact, $F$ combines the change of helmet scale with this change of pose. For a smaller head, the interaction is obtained by applying the $\Delta$ estimator of Section~\ref{sec:design} to $F$ instead of $D$. For the scaled adults, it is therefore the median of $F_{\mathrm{head}}(v) - F_{\mathrm{adult}}(v)$ over common vertices, weighted by the target areas of the smaller head. For the templates, it is the median of the parcel differences, weighted by the adult's parcel areas. Because the interaction is a median of paired differences, it equals neither the difference of the two median contrasts nor $\Delta_{\mathrm{fixed}} - \Delta_{\mathrm{fitted}}$, and Table~S6 gives the non-additivity residual of each head. The interaction does not depend on the assumed OPM noise, because the detectability of the OPM array, which is the same in both helmets, cancels in $F$; it does, however, depend on the modeled Neuromag noise and on the anatomy.

We used three secondary controls of the construction (Section~S5). First, one helmet was fitted at the adult's top-contact gap, << F.rev_cgap_adult_top_gap_mm >>~mm. Second, another helmet was scaled with the head, which scales the gap as well. Third, to compare the heads at an equal OPM site count, the helmet fitted at the adult's gap was used unchanged, but the adult's dense array was subsampled to the number of sites of each smaller head.

