\subsection{Noise model, validation and sensitivity analyses}\label{sec:noise}

All arrays were exposed to the same noise sources in one band of << F.g2_band_range_hz >>~Hz. The noise had three parts: white sensor noise, a cortical background of spontaneous brain activity, and the field of distant sources in the room (the room field). The noise covariance of each array, which the detectability measure takes as known (the oracle covariance), was computed in closed form as
\begin{equation}
\symbf{C} = \symbf{\Sigma}_{\mathrm{n}} + \sigma^{2}\,\symbf{L}\symbf{A}\symbf{L}^{\top} + \symbf{E}\symbf{\Sigma}_{\mathrm{e}}\symbf{E}^{\top}.
\label{eq:C}
\end{equation}
Here, $\symbf{\Sigma}_{\mathrm{n}}$ is the diagonal covariance of white sensor noise. The brain noise enters through the second term, in which $\symbf{L}$ is the lead-field matrix of << F.g2_n_background >> independent cortex-normal background dipoles on a << F.g2_bg_grid_mm >>-mm grid and $\symbf{A}$ is the diagonal matrix of their Voronoi-cell areas. The room field enters through the last term, in which $\symbf{E}$ contains the responses of the array to << F.g2_room_terms|words >> external-field terms (<< F.meth_env_uniform_terms|words >> uniform and << F.meth_env_gradient_terms|words >> linear-gradient components) and $\symbf{\Sigma}_{\mathrm{e}}$ is the covariance of their coefficients. The background is a cortically constrained form of the random-dipole model of spontaneous activity \citep{demunck1992}. Each background dipole has a moment variance of $\sigma^{2}$ times its area ($\sigma^{2}$ = << F.meth_bg_var_per_mm2_nam2 >>~nAm$^{2}$ per mm$^{2}$), and, as for the targets, the cortex within << F.g2_source_skull_clearance_mm >>~mm of the inner skull is omitted from the background.

We fitted two quantities to the sample dataset: the room-field covariance $\symbf{\Sigma}_{\mathrm{e}}$, to the empty-room recording of the dataset, and $\sigma^{2}$, which was set so that the modeled brain noise of the median good gradiometer matched the measured one, a root-mean-square value of << F.meth_bg_calibration_grad_ft_cm >>~fT/cm. Everything else, apart from the datasheet sensor noise of both systems, is predicted rather than fitted. Because these predictions come from the same recording, however, the checks below test the model within that recording, not in another subject or session. We analyzed four noise conditions. The first three were sensor noise only; sensor plus brain noise, the primary condition; and sensor, brain and room noise. To represent interference suppression, the fourth condition was derived from the third by removing the room field from every array with one projection, which removes the array's responses to the room-field terms after weighting each channel by its inverse sensor-noise variance (Eq.~(S1)). We designated this condition, the room field projected out, as the secondary condition before any simulation.

To check the model, we compared its Neuromag covariance with the measured covariance and recomputed Neuromag's detectability with the measured one (Section~S3). For this check, the recording was band-passed to << F.rev_cov_band_hz >>~Hz, and one bad channel was excluded. As the measured covariance, we used the empirical covariance, without regularization or projections, of the << F.rev_cov_n_windows >> pre-stimulus windows (<< F.rev_cov_window_s >>~s; << F.rev_cov_n_baseline_samples >> samples) of the recording. The brain noise of the measured covariance was taken as its difference from the empty-room covariance (<< F.rev_cov_n_empty_room_samples >> samples). Because a covariance estimated from finite data inflates detectability, we removed this inflation with surrogates. We generated << F.rev_cov_n_surrogates >> phase-randomized surrogates of the whole recording, which preserve its spectra and cross-spectra, estimated their covariance from the same samples, and divided the measured detectability of each target by the resulting inflation (median factor << F.rev_cov_det_combined_bias_ratio_3dp >>).

Because the OPM covariance cannot be measured here, we computed the implied OPM/Neuromag ratio under five assumptions about how each system's real noise departs from the model. In scenario A, both systems share the model's error, so the modeled ratio stands. Scenario B scales both cortical backgrounds by $k$ = << F.rev_cov_k_ratio >>, the factor that makes the modeled magnetometer brain noise match the measured one. The other three scenarios replace all or part of Neuromag's modeled noise with measured noise. In scenarios C and D, Neuromag takes its measured covariance; the OPM background is scaled by $k$ in scenario C, whereas the OPM retains its modeled covariance in scenario D. In scenario E, Neuromag takes its measured empty-room noise together with the modeled brain noise.

In each sensitivity analysis, we changed one element of the adult model and recalibrated $\sigma^{2}$. First, the cortex << F.rev_ns_joint_floor_mm >>--<< F.rev_ns_near_rule_mm >>~mm from the inner skull, which we call the near-skull cortex, was added to the background, to the targets, or to both. Second, the OPM noise was made colored (1/$f$ plus white noise, with corners at << F.rev_ns_col_corners_hz >>~Hz) under a matched filter that also whitens across frequency. Third, idealized cardiac and ocular sources were added. Finally, the joint runs combined these changes: the near-skull cortex was added to the background and the OPM noise was given a << F.rev_ns_joint_corner_hz >>-Hz corner under the frequency-whitening detector, with and without the cardiac and ocular sources. Noise specific to OPMs, e.g., cross-talk between neighboring sensors and errors in their gain and sensitive axis, was not modeled, and the distance-independent low-frequency noise of a real OPM (Section~S3.2) was represented only by the colored-noise variants.

\subsection{Simulated interictal spikes}\label{sec:spikes}

The spike simulations were designed to ask whether the detectability difference carries over to the detection of transient events by an idealized detector under the modeled noise. They do not test the noise model. For these simulations, the noise model was run in the time domain, in segments shared by all arrays, and the spike was the spike-wave complex of \citet{hunold2016}, stretched to << F.wr_n_morphologies_words >> morphologies. The data were analyzed with two matched-filter detectors, both whitened with a Ledoit--Wolf covariance \citep{ledoit2004} estimated from << F.g4_null_baseline_minutes >>~min of null data rather than with the oracle covariance.

The first, which we call the oracle detector, knew the source and the time and tested at a per-trial false-positive probability of << F.g4_oracle_alpha >>. The second, the practical detector, knew neither. Instead, it scanned candidate field patterns on a << F.g4_dictionary_spacing_mm >>-mm grid that did not contain the true sources, and its thresholds for a nominal << F.g4_operating_point_per_minute >> false event per minute were fixed in advance (frozen) on independent null data. Note that both detectors are idealized, because they use the true forward model and the simulated waveform family. We interpolated S50, the dipole strength detected in << F.g4_s50_level >> of events, linearly in log strength between the tested strengths. In both spike runs, the smaller heads were placed at top contact in the fixed adult helmet, whereas the adult was placed at its measured head position. At that position, the adult's area-weighted $D$ is << F.g3b_adult_centred_minus_top_d_abs >>~dB higher than at top contact.

In the exploratory run, spikes were placed at << F.g4_n_locations >> locations per anatomy, stratified by orientation and by depth, with << F.g4_locations_per_band >> locations in each of << F.wr_n_depth_bands_words >> depth bands. The bands ranged from << F.g4_band_10_20mm >> to << F.g4_band_45_70mm >>~mm. At each location, << F.g4_n_focal_events_per_location >> focal events of << F.g4_strength_range >>~nAm and patch events from cortical patches of << F.g4_patch_radius_mm >>-mm radius were simulated, each event in its own noise realization and identical for every array. Every comparison was made within one anatomy, and the anatomies are not ranked against one another. In addition, we localized the simulated spikes at << F.loc_n_locations >> locations by equivalent-current-dipole fits and by dynamic statistical parametric mapping (dSPM; \citealp{dale2000}) at the true peak sample, through a single-compartment BEM with a coregistration error of << F.loc_coreg_shift_mm >>~mm and << F.loc_coreg_angle_deg >>°. Every event, detected or not, was used in the paired comparisons of localization error, and a joint measure also required detection (Section~S7).

We then pre-specified a second run, whose endpoint compared the dense OPM array with Neuromag under the practical detector at a nominal << F.g4_operating_point_per_minute >> false event per minute, for focal spikes << F.g4_band_10_20mm >>~mm below the scalp in every anatomy. For this run, we drew << F.meth_confirm_locs >> new locations per anatomy and << F.wr_confirm_replicates_words >> noise replicates per event, the first of which carried the endpoint, and used separate null data for threshold fitting and for rate evaluation. The threshold, set on << F.cf_null_calibration_minutes >>~min of calibration null data, rests on about << F.wr_confirm_calib_expected_events >> false events, which corresponds to a Poisson uncertainty of about << F.wr_confirm_calib_poisson_pct >>. The oracle detector's threshold, in contrast, does not depend on that calibration.

The declared statistic was a two-sided sign-flip test on the per-location differences in detection counts, Holm-adjusted over the anatomies \citep{holm1979} at $\alpha$ = << F.meth_alpha >>. The paired S50 ratio $r$ served as the effect size; a value of $r$ means that the OPM array detects a spike at a strength $100\,(1 - 1/r)$\,\% lower than Neuromag. Whereas the declared test estimated p by Monte Carlo when more than << F.meth_signflip_exact_max >> location differences were nonzero, we report exact p values over all sign patterns, which give the same decisions in all << F.cf_exact_n_families >> families of Holm-adjusted tests (Section~S7). As secondary analyses, we declared a mismatched detector (off-template morphologies, candidate patterns from a single-compartment BEM and a shared coregistration error of << F.wr_confirm_coreg_shift_mm >>~mm and << F.wr_confirm_coreg_angle_deg >>°) and analyzed the site-matched array, the oracle detector and thresholds re-fitted on held-out null data.

\subsection{Statistical analysis}\label{sec:stats}

To preserve the dependence between neighboring targets within a parcel, intervals over targets were computed as << F.g2_ci_level_pct >> percentile bootstrap intervals that resample atlas parcels as units, i.e., the adult's << F.g2_n_parcels >> labels or the parcels of each smaller head. We used << F.meth_boot_g2 >> resamples for the primary comparisons, the noise-model sensitivity analyses and the measured-covariance check, and << F.meth_boot_g2_sens >> for the other sensitivity analyses of the adult and for some secondary constructions of the smaller heads (Table~S16). For $\Delta$ and the interaction, which are medians of paired differences between a smaller head and the adult, the bootstrap resamples the parcels together with their paired differences. In the spike study, locations were the units of resampling and testing.

The parcel intervals describe how sensitive a whole-cortex median is to the parcels it pools, within one anatomy and one noise model. They are neither population confidence intervals nor a measure of the spread of effects over the cortex, and they exclude the larger uncertainties of model error, head placement and between-subject variation. We therefore report whether an interval includes 1 (or 0~dB) as a description, not as a test. The main estimands are listed in Table~\ref{tab:estimands}, and all of them in Table~S2.

Several choices were made after results were known. The exploratory spike endpoint, which was chosen from << F.g4_family_per_opm_array >> comparisons per OPM array, the primary helmet placement and the regions of interest were all named after the whole-cortex results were known. In addition, the helmet fitted at the adult's gap, the check of the noise model against the measured covariance with its five scenarios, and the sensitivity analyses were added later, without changing the model. The model itself, however, was revised while results were known (Section~S8.2). The pre-specified run was declared in a configuration file committed to the public code repository the day before the run, which used the same code and configuration. Only the history of the repository attests this; the run was not registered externally. The number of locations in this run, double the exploratory << F.wr_confirm_pilot_locs_per_band >> per band, was chosen after << F.wr_confirm_pilot_runs_words >> pilot runs in child A. The outputs of these pilot runs were not retained, and their seeds were not reused.

