\section{Results}\label{sec:results}

\subsection{Determinants of the adult comparison}\label{sec:res-adult}

Brain noise reversed the comparison between the dense OPM array and Neuromag (Fig.~\ref{fig:conditions}; Table~\ref{tab:adult}). When only sensor noise was modeled, Neuromag had a higher detectability than either OPM array (Fig.~\ref{fig:conditions}), but when brain noise was added, the dense array had the higher detectability at << F.wr_g2_ahead_n >> of the << F.g2_n_targets >> cortical targets. Over all targets, its median detectability ratio to Neuromag was << F.g2_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> << F.g2_dense_vs_combined_ib_ci >>. In contrast, the site-matched array did not differ clearly from Neuromag (<< F.g2_matched_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> << F.g2_matched_vs_combined_ib_ci >>). The same pattern was observed when the room field was projected out of every array, although the ratio of the site-matched array was then slightly below parity.

Brain noise dominated the noise of both systems (Section~S3). When the room field was included, brain noise accounted for << F.g2_noise_share_dense_brain_pct >> of the in-band variance of an OPM channel in the dense array and for << F.g2_noise_share_squid_mag_brain_pct >> of that of a Neuromag magnetometer. The room field accounted for most of the remaining magnetometer variance (<< F.g2_noise_share_squid_mag_room_pct >>). As a result, brain noise raised the noise of a Neuromag magnetometer about << F.g2_noise_inflation_squid_mag_1dp >>-fold above its sensor floor, but that of an OPM only about << F.g2_noise_inflation_dense_1dp >>-fold, so that the higher sensor noise of the OPM mattered less once brain noise was present.

The ratio also depended on which Neuromag channels served as the comparator and on the SNR measure (Fig.~\ref{fig:conditions}; Table~\ref{tab:adult}). Against the gradiometers alone, the dense array had a larger advantage than against the magnetometers alone (Table~\ref{tab:adult}). Peak-channel SNR is the closest analog of the sensor-level SNRs reported in clinical studies (Section~S1). By this measure, the dense array had the higher SNR when compared with the magnetometers, whereas Neuromag had the higher SNR when its best channel of all << F.g2_squid_channels >> was used. With mean-power SNR, both OPM arrays showed only small advantages (Fig.~\ref{fig:conditions}). Which system a reported number favors thus depends on the Neuromag channels and the SNR measure that it uses.

Of the model variants, the white-noise floor of the OPMs had the largest effect on the ratio (Fig.~\ref{fig:noise}D; Table~\ref{tab:adult}). As the OPM white noise was raised over the range tested, the ratio of the dense array decreased to parity, whereas that of the site-matched array fell below it (Fig.~\ref{fig:noise}D). For the dense array, the break-even OPM noise, i.e., the OPM white noise at which the ratio equals 1 under the modeled noise, was << F.rev_ns_breakeven_dense_vs_combined_ib_asd >> << F.rev_ns_breakeven_dense_vs_combined_ib_asd_ci >>~fT/√Hz (Fig.~\ref{fig:noise}D, diamonds). The site-matched array broke even at << F.rev_ns_breakeven_matched_vs_combined_ib_asd >> << F.rev_ns_breakeven_matched_vs_combined_ib_asd_ci >>~fT/√Hz, an interval that includes the primary OPM noise level. With the room field projected out, the break-even level of the dense array fell to << F.rev_ns_breakeven_dense_vs_combined_proj_asd >> << F.rev_ns_breakeven_dense_vs_combined_proj_asd_ci >>~fT/√Hz (Fig.~\ref{fig:noise}D, open diamonds). That of the site-matched array fell to << F.rev_ns_breakeven_matched_vs_combined_proj_asd >> << F.rev_ns_breakeven_matched_vs_combined_proj_asd_ci >>~fT/√Hz. Note that all of these levels assume the modeled Neuromag noise, which the next section compares with the measured noise.

The other model variants changed the ratio of the dense array less over the ranges tested (Fig.~\ref{fig:conditions}; Table~\ref{tab:adult}). An additional OPM-to-scalp gap reduced the ratio but did not reverse it. Combining an OPM noise of << F.g2_opm_asd_sweep_max >>~fT/√Hz with an additional << F.g2_opm_gap_small_mm >>-mm gap, however, did reverse it (Fig.~\ref{fig:conditions}). With both changes, the ratio was << F.g2_joint_gap3mm_asd30ft_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> << F.g2_joint_gap3mm_asd30ft_dense_vs_combined_ib_ci >>. A triaxial array at the site-matched sites recovered most of the advantage of the dense array, and it still recovered part of that advantage when its tangential axes were twice as noisy (Fig.~\ref{fig:conditions}, triangles). The ratio changed little when the covariance was estimated from a finite number of simulated samples, as many as a minute of data would contain, or when a 3--70-Hz band was used instead of the primary band. Thus, apart from brain noise, the array and the OPM noise floor determined which system had the higher detectability in the adult, whereas the comparator and the SNR measure determined which system a reported SNR favored.

