\subsection{The noise model against measured Neuromag noise}\label{sec:res-noise}

The noise model reproduced some properties of Neuromag's measured noise and missed others (Figs.~S2 and S3). For the magnetometers, the model predicted << F.rev_cov_brain_mag_amp_ratio >> of the measured brain-noise amplitude, a quantity not used in its calibration, and << F.rev_cov_heart_mag_amp_without_ratio >> with the field of the heart removed. When the brain-noise scale was calibrated on a random half of the sites, the predicted gradiometer level of the other half agreed with the measurement nearly by construction (<< F.rev_cov_heldout_random_grad_amp_ratio >>; Fig.~S3B). Over << F.rev_cov_heldout_n_splits >> random splits, the << F.g2_ci_level_pct >> range of this model-to-measured ratio was << F.rev_cov_heldout_random_grad_amp_ci >>. However, the modeled brain noise was too flat from inferior to superior sites. When the model was calibrated on the inferior half of the sites, it predicted << F.rev_cov_heldout_inf_to_sup_grad_amp_ratio >> times the measured gradiometer amplitude of the superior half, and when it was calibrated on the superior half, it predicted << F.rev_cov_heldout_sup_to_inf_grad_amp_ratio >> times that of the inferior half. In addition, the model predicted the per-channel variances poorly, and its magnetometer correlations decreased with distance faster than the measured correlations did. Thus, the measured covariance differed from the modeled one in structure as well as in level.

The measured brain noise also contained variance that the model lacks. This excess, the positive part of the difference between the measured and modeled magnetometer brain-noise covariances, summed to << F.rev_cov_excess_brain_mag_pos_over_model_ratio >> times the summed variance of the modeled brain noise, whereas sampling error alone would produce an excess of << F.rev_cov_excess_brain_mag_pos_over_model_null_range >> times that variance. It was concentrated in a few far-field components (Section~S3). Neuromag's detectability was nevertheless higher with its measured covariance than with the modeled one (Fig.~\ref{fig:noise}A). The median ratio of the two detectabilities was << F.rev_cov_det_combined_corrected_ratio >> << F.rev_cov_det_combined_corrected_ci >>, a factor as large as the whole advantage of the dense array, and it varied by lobe, from << F.rev_cov_det_lobe_temporal_corrected_ratio >> in the temporal lobe to << F.rev_cov_det_lobe_cingulate_corrected_ratio >> in the cingulate. This may appear paradoxical, but it follows from how detectability weighs the noise. Because detectability depends only on the noise along the field pattern of each source after whitening, the measured noise must be lower than the model along the cortical field patterns, with its excess in directions that the whitening down-weights.

What the measured Neuromag noise implies for the OPM depends on the real OPM noise, which no measurement here constrains (Fig.~\ref{fig:noise}B; Table~\ref{tab:adult}). The ratios of both arrays were nearly unchanged when the two systems shared the model's error (scenario A), when both cortical backgrounds were scaled to match the measured magnetometer brain noise (scenario B), or when Neuromag was given its measured empty-room noise and modeled brain noise (scenario E). In contrast, scenario C, in which Neuromag was given its measured covariance and the OPM background was scaled as in scenario B, was the least favorable to the OPM and lowered the ratios of both arrays below parity. When Neuromag was given its measured covariance and the OPM noise remained as modeled (scenario D), the ratio of the dense array decreased to << F.rev_cov_opm_dense_vs_combined_s4_ratio >> << F.rev_cov_opm_dense_vs_combined_s4_ci >>, an interval that includes 1 (Fig.~\ref{fig:noise}B). The ratio of the site-matched array fell to << F.rev_cov_opm_matched_vs_combined_s4_ratio >> << F.rev_cov_opm_matched_vs_combined_s4_ci >>, below parity. Under scenario D, neither OPM array therefore had a clear advantage over Neuromag at the primary OPM noise level.

We did not compute break-even levels under scenario D, but their direction follows from the ratios above. Because Neuromag's measured detectability does not change with the OPM noise, the ratio under this scenario, like the modeled ratio, falls as the OPM noise rises. With a ratio of << F.rev_cov_opm_dense_vs_combined_s4_ratio >> at << F.g2_opm_asd_primary >>~fT/√Hz, the dense array would therefore break even above that level at the point estimate, although the interval of its ratio includes 1. With a ratio of << F.rev_cov_opm_matched_vs_combined_s4_ratio >>, the site-matched array would break even below it, if at all within the tested range. How far either level would move from its modeled value cannot be inferred from the medians alone, because the correction for the measured noise varies across targets.

The model omissions that we tested changed the advantage of the dense array little at the primary OPM noise (Fig.~\ref{fig:noise}C; Table~\ref{tab:adult}). The ratio was unchanged when the near-skull cortex was added as background or as targets (Fig.~\ref{fig:noise}C). Colored OPM noise with the highest corner frequency tested, << F.rev_ns_joint_corner_hz >>~Hz, reduced the ratio slightly, both under the primary detector and under one that also whitens across frequency. Similarly, adding cardiac and ocular sources changed the ratio by only << F.rev_ns_ff_dense_vs_combined_ib_change_pct_range >>.

The joint runs combined the near-skull background with a << F.rev_ns_joint_corner_hz >>-Hz corner of the OPM noise under the frequency-whitening detector (Fig.~\ref{fig:noise}D, dashed). With white OPM noise at << F.g2_opm_asd_primary >>~fT/√Hz, this detector gave the dense array a ratio of << F.rev_ns_joint_white_asd15ft_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> << F.rev_ns_joint_white_asd15ft_dense_vs_combined_ib_ci >>. The joint run reduced it to << F.rev_ns_joint_nofar_asd15ft_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> << F.rev_ns_joint_nofar_asd15ft_dense_vs_combined_ib_ci >> (Table~\ref{tab:adult}), whereas raising the white OPM noise alone to << F.g2_opm_asd_sweep_20 >>~fT/√Hz reduced it more, to << F.rev_ns_joint_white_asd20ft_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> << F.rev_ns_joint_white_asd20ft_dense_vs_combined_ib_ci >>. With both changes together, the ratio was close to parity at << F.g2_opm_asd_sweep_20 >>~fT/√Hz (<< F.rev_ns_joint_nofar_asd20ft_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio_3dp >> << F.rev_ns_joint_nofar_asd20ft_dense_vs_combined_ib_ci_3dp >>, and << F.rev_ns_joint_all_asd20ft_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> << F.rev_ns_joint_all_asd20ft_dense_vs_combined_ib_ci >> with the cardiac and ocular sources added) and below it at << F.g2_opm_asd_sweep_max >>~fT/√Hz (<< F.rev_ns_joint_nofar_asd30ft_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >>; Fig.~\ref{fig:noise}D). Under the same detector, the omitted noise sources thus lowered the ratio less than a rise of the OPM noise from << F.g2_opm_asd_primary >> to << F.g2_opm_asd_sweep_20 >>~fT/√Hz did.

