\subsection{Depth and brain regions}\label{sec:res-depth}

The advantage of the dense array was largest near the scalp and decreased with depth (Fig.~\ref{fig:depth}A). Its ratio decreased from << F.rev_db_dense_vs_combined_ib_10_15_ratio >> << F.rev_db_dense_vs_combined_ib_10_15_ci >> in the << F.g2_depth_bin_10_15_mm >>~mm depth bin to << F.rev_db_dense_vs_combined_ib_25_30_ratio >> << F.rev_db_dense_vs_combined_ib_25_30_ci >> in the << F.g2_depth_bin_25_30_mm >>~mm bin and then changed little, remaining at << F.g2_depth_dense_vs_combined_ib_35_60_range >> from the << F.g2_depth_bin_35_40_mm >> to the << F.g2_depth_bin_55_60_mm >>~mm bin. Even the deepest targets thus kept a small advantage, and every interval in these bins excluded parity. The site-matched array, in contrast, had an equal-SNR depth, as in the spherical analysis, even with sensor plus brain noise. Its intervals were above 1 down to the << F.rev_db_matched_vs_combined_ib_ci_above1_through_bin >>~mm bin and below 1 from the << F.rev_db_matched_vs_combined_ib_ci_below1_from_bin >>~mm bin, so that Neuromag had the higher detectability for the deeper sources.

