Projecting out the room field reduced the ratios of both OPM arrays at depth (Fig.~\ref{fig:depth}A, dashed). The dense array then also had an equal-SNR depth, with bin medians below 1 from the << F.rev_db_dense_vs_combined_proj_median_below1_from_bin >>~mm bin, although its intervals were entirely below 1 only in the << F.rev_db_dense_vs_combined_proj_bins_ci_below1 >>~mm bin. The equal-SNR depth of the site-matched array became shallower, with bin medians below 1 from the << F.rev_db_matched_vs_combined_proj_median_below1_from_bin >>~mm bin and intervals below 1 from the << F.rev_db_matched_vs_combined_proj_ci_below1_from_bin >>~mm bin. Whether an OPM array keeps its advantage at depth thus depends on how the room field is treated.

The ratio of the dense array decreased with depth whether or not brain noise was present. The decrease was already present with sensor noise alone (Fig.~\ref{fig:depth}A, dotted) and in the peak field itself, i.e., in the signal without noise (Fig.~\ref{fig:depth}B), whereas adding brain noise multiplied the ratio by a nearly constant factor in every bin (<< F.g2_brainmult_dense_vs_combined_range >>). Together, these observations suggest that the decrease arose from the source--sensor geometry rather than from the noise.

The dense array had the higher detectability almost everywhere on the cortex, most of all on the lateral convexity (Fig.~\ref{fig:maps}). The regional results, however, are exploratory point estimates under the modeled noise. Among the lobes, the advantage of the dense array was largest in the frontal lobe (<< F.g2_lobe_frontal_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> << F.g2_lobe_frontal_dense_vs_combined_ib_ci >>; Fig.~\ref{fig:regions}A). The smallest advantages were found in the cingulate (<< F.g2_lobe_cingulate_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> << F.g2_lobe_cingulate_dense_vs_combined_ib_ci >>) and the insula (<< F.g2_lobe_insula_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >>; Fig.~\ref{fig:regions}A). Of the regions chosen from the epilepsy literature, the precentral gyrus favored the dense array most (<< F.reg_precentral_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >>) and the parahippocampal cortex least (<< F.reg_parahippocampal_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >>; Fig.~\ref{fig:regions}A), although another << F.reg_dk_dense_vs_combined_ib_below_parahippocampal_count >> atlas parcels, mostly cingulate and medial, had a smaller advantage than the parahippocampal cortex.

