The site-matched array had its lowest ratios on ventral and polar surfaces (Fig.~\ref{fig:maps}, bottom). Its ratio was << F.reg_dk_frontalpole_matched_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> at the frontal pole and << F.reg_dk_temporalpole_matched_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> at the temporal pole. In the medial and lateral orbitofrontal cortex, it was << F.reg_dk_medialorbitofrontal_matched_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> and << F.reg_dk_lateralorbitofrontal_matched_vs_combined_ib_ratio >>, respectively. Among the regions from the literature, the ratio of the site-matched array was lowest in the mesial temporal cortex, the parahippocampal and entorhinal parcels (<< F.reg_mesial_temporal_matched_vs_combined_ib_ratio >>).

However, the regional ranking of the dense array changed when Neuromag was given its measured noise (scenario D). Under this scenario, the ratio of the dense array became << F.wr_cov_s4_dense_lobe_temporal_ratio >> in the temporal lobe, the highest of any lobe, and << F.wr_cov_s4_dense_lobe_frontal_ratio >> in the frontal lobe. The ratio fell below 1 in the cingulate (<< F.wr_cov_s4_dense_lobe_cingulate_ratio >>) and the parietal lobe (<< F.wr_cov_s4_dense_lobe_parietal_ratio >>). The depth-bin medians of the dense array were also below 1 from the << F.wr_cov_s4_dense_depth_first_below1_label >>~mm bin. Overall, the regional ranking depended on the noise model as well as on the array.

\subsection{Smaller heads in the fixed adult helmet}\label{sec:res-heads}

Every principal smaller head showed a larger OPM advantage than the adult when placed in the fixed adult helmet (Fig.~\ref{fig:heads}; Table~\ref{tab:heads}). At top contact, $D$ was << F.g3b_principal_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_range >>~dB in these heads, compared with << F.g3b_adult_dense_vs_combined_ib_d >> << F.g3b_adult_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_ci >>~dB in the adult (Fig.~\ref{fig:heads}A), and the paired change from the adult, $\Delta$, was << F.g3b_principal_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >>~dB (Fig.~\ref{fig:heads}B). This excess persisted when the variance of the cortical background per unit area was halved or doubled: the $D$ of every principal head still exceeded that of the adult, by << F.g3b_principal_bg0p5_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_minus_adult_range >> and << F.g3b_principal_bg2_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_minus_adult_range >>~dB, respectively (differences of medians, without intervals). The median magnetometer-to-scalp gaps of these heads were also larger, << F.g3b_principal_top_gap_range >>~mm, compared with << F.g3b_adult_top_gap >>~mm in the adult (Fig.~\ref{fig:fit}A). The larger OPM advantage of these heads was thus accompanied by a larger gap, which the next section separates from head size.

