The placement of the head in the helmet, an uncertainty that the parcel-bootstrap intervals do not include, mattered as much as head size. Over the source-blind placements of the fixed helmet, the change from the adult in the school-age scaled adult spanned << F.rev_cgap_school_band_dense_vs_combined_ib_range >>~dB (Fig.~\ref{fig:fit}B). This range, computed from differences of medians, is wider than the parcel interval of the change at top contact, << F.g3b_school_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_ci >>~dB. In each of the << F.wr_n_principal_words >> principal heads, the change remained positive at every placement (Fig.~\ref{fig:fit}B, gray ranges). Over the same placements, the adult's own $D$ spanned << F.g3b_adult_family_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_span >>~dB. The << F.wr_n_provisional_words >> heads reported separately in Section~S10 had changes of << F.g3b_provisional_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >>~dB. The smallest changes, << F.g3b_children_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >>~dB, were observed in the << F.wr_n_children_words >> school-aged children. A larger OPM advantage in a smaller head may therefore reflect the fit of the adult helmet as much as the head itself.

\subsection{Helmet fit versus head size}\label{sec:res-fit}

Relative to the fitted helmet, the fixed adult helmet was less favorable to Neuromag in every principal smaller head than in the adult (Fig.~\ref{fig:fit}C; Table~\ref{tab:heads}). This interaction was << F.wr_cgap_principal_interaction_range >>~dB in the principal heads, with every interval above zero; the two scaled adults defined the extremes of this range, and the templates were intermediate, at << F.wr_cgap_principal_templates_interaction_range >>~dB. In the << F.wr_n_provisional_words >> heads reported separately in Section~S10, the interaction was << F.wr_cgap_provisional_interaction_range >>~dB, and only the interval of child C included zero (<< F.rev_cgap_child_c_fitted_combined_ib_interaction >> << F.rev_cgap_child_c_fitted_combined_ib_interaction_ci >>~dB). Part of the interaction comes from the adult, whose own contrast between the two helmets was negative (<< F.rev_cgap_adult_fixed_minus_fitted_combined_ib >>~dB). This negative contrast, consistent with the adult's larger median gap in the fitted helmet (<< F.rev_cgap_adult_gap_mm >>~mm) than at top contact (<< F.rev_cgap_adult_fixed_gap_mm >>~mm), is the main reason why the interaction exceeded the within-head contrast of each principal head (<< F.rev_cgap_principal_fixed_minus_fitted_combined_ib_range >>~dB). About half of the adult's contrast, however, arises from the construction of the fitted helmet itself: between top contact and a helmet fitted at the adult's top-contact gap, which have the same median gap, it was << F.rev_cgap_adult_fixed_minus_fittedtop_combined_ib >> << F.rev_cgap_adult_fixed_minus_fittedtop_combined_ib_ci >>~dB (Section~S5).

