When the helmet was fitted to each head and each head had its own dense array, most of the extra OPM advantage of the smaller heads disappeared (Fig.~\ref{fig:fit}B; Table~\ref{tab:heads}). With both the smaller head and the adult in helmets fitted at the adult's gap, $\Delta$ was << F.rev_cgap_principal_fitted_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >>~dB, with interval bounds of << F.wr_cgap_principal_fitted_delta_ci_bounds_range >>~dB (Table~\ref{tab:heads}). Every interval included zero except that of the school-age scaled adult, whose lower bound was just above zero. When the helmet was instead fitted at the adult's top-contact gap, these heads retained a change of << F.wr_cgap_principal_fittedtop_vs_top_range >>~dB relative to the adult at top contact (Section~S5). When the room field was projected out, the change in the fitted helmet was negative in every principal head (<< F.rev_cgap_principal_fitted_dense_vs_combined_proj_delta_range >>~dB), and only << F.wr_cgap_principal_fitted_proj_delta_n_ci_includes_zero_words >> of the << F.wr_n_principal_words >> intervals included zero (Table~S6). In the fitted helmet, the change from the adult therefore amounted to a few hundredths of a decibel, and it became negative once the room field was projected out.

Because the fitted helmet changes the gap, the placement and the coverage of the sensors together, none of these quantities apportions the effects exactly. At equal OPM site count, the change in the fitted helmet increased to << F.rev_cgap_principal_fitted_eqsites_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >>~dB (Table~\ref{tab:heads}). The corresponding changes were << F.rev_cgap_principal_fitted_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >>~dB with the array of each head and << F.g3b_principal_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >>~dB in the fixed helmet. Thus, the fitted helmet removed almost all of the extra advantage only because each smaller head had its own, smaller dense array. The comparison suggests that, in the fitted helmet, the scaled adults and templates retained a gain of a few tenths of a decibel from their smaller size, a gain that their fewer OPM sites offset.

\subsection{Simulated interictal spikes}\label{sec:res-spikes}

Under the modeled noise, with idealized detectors and at a nominal false-event rate, the dense array detected simulated superficial spikes at a lower strength than Neuromag in the adult and in every principal smaller head (Fig.~\ref{fig:confirm}A; Table~\ref{tab:spikes}). The declared endpoint, the S50 ratio Neuromag/OPM of the pre-specified run (S50 being the dipole strength at which << F.g4_s50_level >> of the events are detected), was << F.cf_adult_principal_dense_vs_combined_practical_ratio_range >> in these anatomies, and every location-bootstrap interval was above 1 (Table~\ref{tab:spikes}). Spikes << F.cf_band >>~mm deep were thus detected at << F.cf_adult_principal_dense_vs_combined_practical_reduction_pct_range >> lower strength with the dense array. In the smaller heads, this advantage includes an effect of helmet fit: they were placed at top contact in the fixed adult helmet, whose fit raises their modeled OPM advantage (Section~\ref{sec:res-fit}), and spikes were not simulated in the fitted helmet. As expected when detectability is higher, the declared sign-flip test favored the dense array in each of these anatomies (p << F.cf_adult_principal_dense_vs_combined_practical_p_holm_max|replace("<", "< ") >> in each of these << F.cf_adult_principal_n_anatomies|words >>, Holm-adjusted over the << F.meth_n_anatomies|words >> declared anatomies; Table~S11). In the adult, the S50 ratio (<< F.cf_adult_dense_vs_combined_practical_ratio >>) was of the order of the detectability ratio at these depths (<< F.rev_db_dense_vs_combined_ib_15_20_ratio >>--<< F.rev_db_dense_vs_combined_ib_10_15_ratio >>; Fig.~\ref{fig:depth}A), so the detectability advantage carried over to the detection of these spikes.

